\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1800\margr1800\margt1440\margb1440\f0\fs24\cf0
\pard\plain \fi720\sl288\slmult1\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 There are people who believe, against all sense, that fine art is a sign of higher culture. They hang paintings on walls as if venerating gods, as though art has something to do with the truth. But these people are idiots. Art has nothing to do with the truth. Art is seduction. An artist is seduced by themselves, and through their art, they seduce others.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 It\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s arrogance, mostly. Everyone thinks they can see the essential features of the world more clearly than everyone else, but only an artist has the arrogance to make a poem or painting to try and prove it. \u8220\'93I paint what I see,\u8221\'94 they say, or worse: \u8220\'93I write what I know\u8221\'94. As if anyone knows anything. As if it were possible to capture the magnitude of a thing like love, or awe, or beauty by piling paint upon itself. And this gets to the truth: the attempt to capture beauty only kills it, because everything is connected to everything else, and to capture a thing, one must cut it out. And so every \u8216\'91beautiful\u8217\'92 painting is a carcass. And more: societies which value the arts are death cults.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri burped.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He took a deep swig of his burgeon hooch and let the drink carry his thoughts further into this hole, because the seduction angle neatly explained why every artist who has ever tried to capture the beauty of nature had failed. Compared to this sunset which colored the world in dramatic pinks and purples, a countryside trimmed with gold, the painting of the most adventurous master was child\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s play.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 But even as he thought these things, he was skeptical of them. Conscious thought was a tool for midwits. If he weren\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t careful, he might start taking himself seriously and become a philosopher. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Then again, what was the point of getting wasted if he didn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t play with the thoughts his mind shat out as it fell apart? He had to do }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1\cf1 something}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1  while he waited for the hooch to hollow him out.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He took another big swig of the hooch and let the beauty of the pinkish clouds work their magic on him. These were }{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 tall, mountainous clouds, the kind that swallowed landscape paintings whole, the kind that cast broad shadows down across the world to pull the viewer through the distance, as they pulled him now, all the way out to the sparkling swathe of purple called the Shalbrum sea. The sun was setting down for a swim. It dipped its belly, and a}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ll those chalky purples and pinks caught fire, and the sparkle on the sea\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s surface got so bright he had to squint. That was a thing about the high from these down-valley burgeons\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 they could make textures slide and edges bleed, but the chief effect was that anything bright would sparkle: the sunlit cloudrims, the seashine, every bright line and specular reflection would tighten up and sparkle like a twinkling star.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The stars themselves?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 In about a hand, when the sun was gone, and the dark had come, and his mind\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s eye was diving deep into this burgeon binge, all the pinpricked stars would bleed their white into the night, bright streams dripping down into the sea, and his vision would swirl it all together into one solid, flexing sheet that pulsed and breathed like an animal.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He licked his lips, then tossed his emptied hooch skin into his handcart.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He rummaged around the cart, looking for the other skin of hooch that he knew was hiding under the huge mess of rolled up paintings. His hands trembled as they searched for it, because it was his last skin of burgeon hooch, and he needed it. By midday tomorrow, he\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d be all out.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Then\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 And then\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Then he\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d be bricked into sobriety.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 And it wasn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t even good hooch!}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 More sugar water than hooch.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 And he didn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t like these down-valley burgeons}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 . Their waxy capsule leaves split too easy, meaning the insides of the bulb dried out before he could get to them in the ways that he wanted to. The only way to have them was to spike a hooch.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He eventually found the skin of hooch nesting in between two twine-wrapped jars of powdered pigment. He pulled the skin out, popped the cork and took a swig. Then he took a longer swig and continued spiraling over the fact that he\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d ever picked up a paintbrush.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Because what was a painting, really?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Paint that tree\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 that tall, stout, tree whose bushy head leaned over the seaward side of this dusty country lane. If he were to paint that tree, he\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d be forced to reduce all its leaves to flecks of sunset green. A living leaf with all its complexity, its pattern, its anastomosing veins pumped full of vital fluids, its green birth and brown death, its fall from the branch, its decay into dirt, that whole nested universe, reduced to a fleck of green. Reduced, because again, art is about seduction. Look at this, and not that. In some way, a work of art is defined by everything it seeks to hide. Paint an oasis, and look, there\u8217\'92s a naked woman bathing in the mossy water. Focus the viewer\u8217\'92s gaze by sharpening the droplets of jade that gleam on her cinnamon skin. The leaf? Feather its edge into the sky. Blame it on the effects of atmospheric haze. Better yet, don\u8217\'92t paint the leaf, paint bunches of leafy shapes. If the composition would be improved by removing the tree entirely, then please! From almost any vantage, reality could use improvement.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 What kind of person sees the wonder of the world and thinks, \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93People ought to know about }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1\cf1 my}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1  interpretation\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94? As if Solraheen, the god of creation, was not already speaking. And somehow other people encourage this arrogance. Crowds of enablers herd themselves into Lordly galleries to be seduced. Immeasurable wonder is right outside, but they\u8217\'92d all rather see it cornered, killed, flayed, with choice sections of its hide stitched into pretty frames, then put on display. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Yes, the Hrkan world is a death cult.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Go and paint all this,\u8221\'94 Mandri said to the mosquitoes and sighing grass as he swept his arm over the sunset. \u8220\'93It\u8217\'92s not possible. Take as long as you like. Take a lifetime. Take ten lifetimes! Then\u8230\'85 and then\u8230\'85\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 And then he forgot what he was on about because a man was standing about thirty yards away in a field.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 And that field was another thing that no artist had ever captured. It was ugly. True ugliness had never been put to paint.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 This field on the other side of this ramshackle fence was ugliest spot of land in all the world. It was a gentle hill, and it had been impaled with so many bean poles that it looked a bit like the hill had picked a fight with a porcupine. These poles were wrapped with beanstalks, Basag beans, named after Basag, the martyred pilgrim who\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d been tied to a pole by demons. And there shallow pits all over, divots, each stained with an irregular ring of salt.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri ambled of the country lane, turning his handcart with some difficulty because he had to lift the damn thing up to turn it. Broken swing gate.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Don\u8217\'92t bother!\u8221\'94 the old farmer called. \u8220\'93Whatever ya sellin\u8217\'92, I ain\u8217\'92t buyin\u8217\'92!\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri, still struggling with his cart, pretended not to hear. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Hello!\u8221\'94 he called back. And with his cart straightened, he proceeded down the farmers lane with a salesman\u8217\'92s smile, while the skinny wheels of his cart squeaked for want of grease. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Licensed vagrancy required tact and guile, as well as willingness to deceive. He lacked a natural these virtues. And to be painting peddler, which was his official trade, . His route through life had given him , and the only way to make money He did it because he had, long ago}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Desperate to be ambushed by some incredible work that . The unfortunate reality is that the vast, vast majority of paintings produced in the past few hundred years were , and over the past year he\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d come to the only things worth seeing were the things that burgeon. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 They were down-valley burgeons, decent stuff, but the waxy capsule leaves were liable to split. The insides would dry out before he could get to them in the ways that he wanted to. But, that was all Geem had, so he bought them.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1  It was an ox. It was the same damned yellow-headed ox from Vigram\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s leech farm.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 What were the chances?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 This far out?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Mruuuuu,\u8221\'94 the ox said when it noticed him gawking. It sauntered through burgeon addled haze, dewlap flapping. The skip in its step suggested it recognized him. And as it came into focus, the state of its distress became all too clear: red scrapes all over, courtesy of the thorns surrounding Vigram\u8217\'92s farm. Purple bruises splotched its armpits. The rest of its shaven body was a grotesque mosaic of little blood rings where leeches used to be. Those leeches still attached were concentrated on its haunches, with a few suckling on its belly.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The poor thing was obviously unable to care for itself, and so it was coming to him for help. It had no idea where it was. Absent a miracle, it was going to die.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s heart went out to the creature.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I can\u8217\'92t do anything for you,\u8221\'94 he said, but even as he said it, he knew it wasn\u8217\'92t true. He could do something. He could scrape off those leeches. He could lead the beast to water. He could go to the extreme and devote the rest of his meaningless life to caring for that ox. He wasn\u8217\'92t going to do that, but he could! He could. Should he? The answer was an obvious no, because this was only an ox. Thousands of oxes just like it were being butchered and eaten throughout the world. He\u8217\'92d eaten plenty of beef himself. Helping this ox and not the others would make him a hypocrite. There was a whole leech farm full of animals that needed saving down the road, and he hadn\u8217\'92t spared one thought for helping any of them. Were they somehow less worthy than this ox? Was he somehow responsible for the life of every living being in all creation? Where was the line?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Technically, he did own this ox. That purchase involved a tacit of assumption of responsibility. But, he hadn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t actually expected to ever see the beast again. Why was it here? How? It had wandered off Vigram\u8217\'92s farm, and just so happened to find his own?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He suspected a setup of some sort. Fate was never so neat. Or, maybe the ox itself was intelligent. Some say newborn gods can take the form of animals.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He cast a skeptical eye upon the ox as it picked its path around the crooked bean poles. It was clearly too dumb to be a god. And too weak. Poor thing, the whites of its eyes were red from being forced to sniff all that freeweed smoke.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 It came to a stop just feet from him. The fluid inside the fat sun blisters on its back continued to swish around.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Damn,\u8221\'94 Mandri muttered.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The beast really needed help.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 But, so did he. That was the way of the world. Creatures had to take care themselves. There just wasn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t enough concern to go around.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri sipped his hooch. He held his hand up to bless the ox. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I release you from my service,\u8221\'94 he said in regal tones.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He took another sip. The burgeonsight had the short yellow hairs on the ox\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s head swirling. He sipped his hooch again and downed the dregs. He had more hooch sitting inside his hornscrub tree and wanted it.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Stop looking at me like that,\u8221\'94 he said. \u8220\'93I can\u8217\'92t do anything for you.\u8221\'94 }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Now that he\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d said what he said, it could not be taken back.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He made for his tree.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The damned ox followed him.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He sat down in the crook of his tree. The ox sat down nearby\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 it lay on its belly like it hadn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t done in over a week. It\u8217\'92s heavy ribs squished a few leeches.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Do we know each other?\u8221\'94 Mandri asked.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The ox stared back at him, full of bovine dumbness. Its big, dozing breaths rolled its bleeding hide back and forth over its sagging spine. Now that Mandri was positively high on burgeons, the sight of that rolling hide was a sight to see. The skin appeared to boil.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He nestled deeper into the tree trunk\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s embrace as the nightly breeze caressed his cheek. He sighed a little half-sipped, absent sigh, the kind of sigh that he\u8217\'92d make whenever he was ready for a restful sleep. He let his eyelids slouch as he watched flies buzz in and out of ox\u8217\'92s snout. The ox snorted, blowing those fuzzy black dots out of his great big nostrils. The flies zipped right back in to lick at the only reliable source of moisture that they could find in this godforsaken field.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He continued along the road, stomping his way through the muddy wheel-ruts, slapping away the flies, humming nursery rhymes, trying anything to distract himself from the thought that he only had one last , and no real prospects for a client to provide him with more.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He didn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t put much stock in philosophies dreamed up while high on burgeons, but in private, eyes closed, he indulged. And who knows? He could see the pattern everywhere: Inhale, Exhale; Hot, Cold; Come, Go. Perhaps the being at the bottom of everything was a pair of lungs, and all of existence shared the same extensive breath, a cosmic exhale motivating all life.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He began to dream about a cosmic inhale, where everything moved, and talked, and breathed in reverse, all the way back to the beginning of time.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1}